Here's a robust implementation of `isAdmin()` that handles common ways a user's role might be stored:

```php
<?php

/**
 * Determine whether the given user has the admin role.
 *
 * Supports users with either:
 *   - a single role:      $user->role = 'admin'
 *   - multiple roles:     $user->roles = ['editor', 'admin']
 *
 * @param object|null $user
 * @return bool
 */
function isAdmin($user): bool
{
    if (!is_object($user)) {
        return false;
    }

    $adminRole = 'admin';

    // Single role stored as a string property
    if (isset($user->role) && is_string($user->role)) {
        if (strcasecmp(trim($user->role), $adminRole) === 0) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    // Multiple roles stored as an array property
    if (isset($user->roles) && is_array($user->roles)) {
        foreach ($user->roles as $role) {
            if (is_string($role) && strcasecmp(trim($role), $adminRole) === 0) {
                return true;
            }
        }
    }

    return false;
}
```

### Example usage

```php
$user1 = (object) ['name' => 'Alice', 'role' => 'admin'];
$user2 = (object) ['name' => 'Bob', 'roles' => ['editor', 'Admin']];
$user3 = (object) ['name' => 'Carol', 'role' => 'subscriber'];

var_dump(isAdmin($user1)); // bool(true)
var_dump(isAdmin($user2)); // bool(true)
var_dump(isAdmin($user3)); // bool(false)
